<section class="wrap assessment-signals">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">When a walkthrough helps</p>
    <h2>Clarity before the equipment conversation.</h2>
    <p>An assessment is useful when the problem is visible but the right combination of placement, reuse, replacement, and timing is not.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="assessment-signal-grid">
    <article><span>01</span><h3>Camera placement is uncertain</h3><p>You know the areas that matter but not which views, heights, or locations will be useful.</p></article>
    <article><span>02</span><h3>The system came with the property</h3><p>You want to understand what is installed and what may be worth keeping or changing.</p></article>
    <article><span>03</span><h3>Improvements need an order</h3><p>You want to separate the first priority from additions that can reasonably wait.</p></article>
    <article><span>04</span><h3>The space or operation is changing</h3><p>You want to think through coverage before committing to equipment or a layout.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="assessment-walk">
  <div class="wrap assessment-walk-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">What the walkthrough considers</p>
      <h2>From the property edge to the daily routine.</h2>
    </div>
    <ol>
      <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Goals &amp; concerns</strong><p>Start with what you need to see, protect, understand, or simplify.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Approaches &amp; sightlines</strong><p>Look at exterior movement, mounting conditions, light, distance, and obstructions.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Entries &amp; active areas</strong><p>Follow how people, deliveries, customers, and staff move through the property.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>04</span><div><strong>Existing equipment</strong><p>Identify what is present and discuss where reuse or change may make sense.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Priorities &amp; constraints</strong><p>Separate what matters now from what can wait and what the property can support.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap assessment-outcome">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">The useful outcome</p>
    <h2>A clearer next step—not a larger shopping list.</h2>
  </div>
  <div>
    <p>The conversation should narrow the problem. You may need a focused camera project, a broader security plan, a phased approach, or no change in an area that is already working.</p>
    <div class="assessment-decisions">
      <span><b>Keep</b>Equipment or coverage that still serves the goal</span>
      <span><b>Improve</b>Placement, access, or operation that creates friction</span>
      <span><b>Add</b>A new layer only where it has a clear purpose</span>
      <span><b>Phase</b>Later priorities that do not need to happen now</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

